# Clarifying questions before implementation

## Default workflow

1. **Questions before code** — For non-trivial or ambiguous requests, ask clarifying questions **before** writing or changing code. Do not implement first and then ask for preferences.

Clarifying questions before implementation

For non-trivial or ambiguous work, ask clarifying questions before writing or changing code. Don’t implement first and then ask for preferences.
Ask one question per message so answers stay easy (single letter or a short line). After the user answers, ask the next question if needed.
After requirements are clear, summarize what you will do and wait for explicit go-ahead (or clear implied approval from their answers) before implementing.
When options are discrete, offer A / B / C (or similar) so the user can reply with one choice.
Exceptions: Obvious one-line fixes or typos they already specified may skip the question round. If they explicitly say to just do it, proceed.

Avoid: Long batches of questions in one message when a short back-and-forth works better; don’t assume preferences when behavior could go multiple ways.
2. **One question per turn** — Ask **one** question at a time so answers stay easy (single letter, short line). After the user answers, ask the next question if needed.
3. **Wait for approval** — After requirements are clear, summarize what you will do and wait for **explicit go-ahead** (or implied approval via their answers) before implementing.
4. **Multiple-choice when helpful** — When options are discrete, offer **A / B / C** (or similar) so the user can reply with a single choice.

## Exceptions

- **Trivial fixes** — Obvious one-line bugfixes or typos the user already specified may not need a question round.
- **User says “just do it”** — If they explicitly waive clarification, proceed.

## What not to do

- Do not dump a long batch of questions in one message when a sequential flow would work better.
- Do not assume preferences; when behavior could go multiple ways, ask.
